{
"segments": [
{
"s": 0.0,
"e": 6.08,
"text": "One of the biggest things breathwork has taught me is how often I go into my head to avoid feeling what's happening in my body."
},
{
"s": 7.54,
"e": 15.84,
"text": "For years I struggled with anxiety and I spent so much time trying to understand it. Reading books, going to therapy, doing all sorts of self-development work."
},
{
"s": 16.54,
"e": 23.9,
"text": "One of my biggest breakthroughs was realizing I didn't actually need to get rid of the anxiety. I could feel anxious and still get on with my life."
},
{
"s": 23.9,
"e": 32.8,
"text": "But strangely I never really applied that lesson to other emotions. Whenever something uncomfortable came up, I'd immediately start analyzing it."
},
{
"s": 33.14,
"e": 37.56,
"text": "Why am I feeling this? Is it something from my childhood? Is there something I need to work through?"
},
{
"s": 38.58,
"e": 47.7,
"text": "And while understanding our emotions can be really useful, I've started to realize that sometimes all this thinking is actually a way of avoiding the feeling itself."
},
{
"s": 48.58,
"e": 56.0,
"text": "I remember having a really powerful breathwork experience in Bali where some pretty uncomfortable feelings started coming up."
},
{
"s": 56.36,
"e": 64.66,
"text": "Immediately my mind wanted to understand what was happening. Why am I feeling this? Where is it coming from? I kept catching myself doing this."
},
{
"s": 65.62,
"e": 72.98,
"text": "Every time I'd remind myself to come back into my body. And it made me realize how often we do this in everyday life."
},
{
"s": 72.98,
"e": 80.92,
"text": "We overthink, distract ourselves, keep busy, sometimes even lash out at someone. Take people pleasing for example."
},
{
"s": 81.6,
"e": 87.48,
"text": "You send someone a message and afterwards you start worrying about how they've interpreted it. Maybe they think you're rude."
},
{
"s": 88.7,
"e": 92.46,
"text": "So you send another message to explain yourself or make sure everything's okay."
},
{
"s": 93.56,
"e": 101.04,
"text": "But sometimes you're not really sending that second message because anything needs fixing. You're sending it because you want that uncomfortable feeling to go away."
},
{
"s": 101.86,
"e": 110.44,
"text": "And I've started noticing this in myself in much smaller moments too. Like when I'm having my morning coffee and start thinking about everything I need to do that day."
},
{
"s": 111.38,
"e": 121.6,
"text": "I can feel the familiar tension building. And rather than trying to immediately calm myself down or think my way out of it, practicing just noticing it."
},
{
"s": 122.42,
"e": 127.66,
"text": "Where do I actually feel this in my body and can I let it be there without needing to do anything about it?"
},
{
"s": 127.66,
"e": 130.74,
"text": "Can I get on with my day even if it doesn't disappear?"
},
{
"s": 131.72,
"e": 136.72,
"text": "Because I'm starting to realize that sometimes I don't need to understand fix or get rid of what I'm feeling."
},
{
"s": 137.6,
"e": 142.48,
"text": "Sometimes I just need to let myself feel it. And I don't need to feel good all the time to be okay."
}
],
